1
00:00:01,890 --> 00:00:02,799
Done.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:04,220
Is it, though?

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:07,813
And then you go back and check every step — yourself.

4
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:11,320
Vacant. Install once. Work as usual.

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:14,920
pi, Claude Code, OpenCode, Codex — just as they are.

6
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:17,640
Contracts? Config? Keys? — None of it.

7
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:20,320
Every step, quietly signed and chained.

8
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:24,200
When nothing's wrong, it's invisible — not one byte of your requests changes.

9
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:25,629
Until the agent says: “Done.”

10
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,220
In that moment, it reads only the record — five checks.

11
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,670
File written? Failure skipped? Test claims match?

12
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:35,470
Numbers sourced? Named file opened?

13
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:38,920
If not, it goes back to be fixed — before it reaches you.

14
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:43,720
Delivered with a note: what was checked, what changed, what's still unverified.

15
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:48,190
With a 15-turn cap, accuracy went from 42% to 53%.

16
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:51,540
What it recovered: answers called done, but never written.

17
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:55,820
It doesn't judge right from wrong. It makes sure every step has grounds.

18
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:59,478
Vacant. Every delivery, grounded.
